package mop;

import javax.crypto.spec.SecretKeySpec;

import br.unb.cic.mop.eh.*;
import br.unb.cic.mop.jca.util.ConscryptAliasTable;
import br.unb.cic.mop.PredicateStore;
import br.unb.cic.mop.PredicateVerdict;
import br.unb.cic.mop.Property;

/**
 * SecretKeySpec
 *
 * A JavaMOP specification of the correct usage of the
 * javax.crypto.spec.SecretKeySpec.
 *
 * @see https://github.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/blob/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/SecretKeySpec.crysl
 */
SecretKeySpecSpec(SecretKeySpec secretKeySpec) {

   // The envelope's quoting rule (design D-3): a null value is the empty string, a single
   // quote is escaped so it cannot close the field early, and the value is capped so a
   // pathological argument cannot push the record past the logcat payload limit.
   private String q(String s) {
       if (s == null) {
           return "";
       }
       String value = s.length() > 512 ? s.substring(0, 512) : s;
       return value.replace("'", "\\'");
   }

   // D-15 restores the algorithm clause this file had lost. The api30 SecretKeySpec rule
   // states no algorithm constraint at all, so conforming to it meant deleting the list the
   // monitor already had -- the audit's clearest case of the withdrawn oracle destroying a
   // check rather than widening one. SecretKeySpec.crysl states
   // `keyAlgorithm in {"AES", "HmacSHA256", "HmacSHA384", "HmacSHA512"}`; the six HMAC[-/]SHA*
   // entries are the frozen jca's spelling variants of the last three.
   List<String> algorithms = Arrays.asList("AES", "HMACSHA256", "HMACSHA384", "HMACSHA512",
           "HMAC-SHA256", "HMAC/SHA256", "HMAC-SHA384", "HMAC/SHA384", "HMAC/SHA512", "HMAC-SHA512");

   SecretKeySpec spec;

   // The second place of `generatedKey[this, alg]` (SecretKeySpec.cryptsl:41). The rule
   // binds `alg` in both constructors, so both events stage it beside the object, and the
   // handler writes the pair. It travels in a field for the same reason `spec` does: a
   // handler is generated as a no-argument method and sees no event parameter.
   String specAlgorithm;

   // One event over `SecretKeySpec(byte[], String)`, where there used to be two
   // split by the sign of a guard. The negated twin `c3` matched the same call with
   // the same `args(keyMaterial, keyAlgorithm)` and named no state in the `ere`, so
   // one bad construction drew two reports: a body accusation and an ordering one
   // about an ordering the rule accepts, because an event outside the automaton gets
   // a transition row that sends every state to the fail state. The fusion kept the
   // first and removed the second (task 3.4).
   //
   // Task 4.10 moved the read to the new store, which closed the last window the
   // migration had left open while the read still asked about `randomized`. This pass
   // changes which predicate it asks about, and the corpus trace SecretKeySpecSpec.txt
   // is where the two answers part company: material an observed SecureRandom filled
   // satisfied the old question and does not satisfy the rule's.
   //
   // The verdict is three-valued and the two accusing outcomes are different claims.
   // NOT_OBSERVED says no preparation of this array was ever seen -- on Android as
   // often a reach limit of the instrumentation as a misuse -- and it is the one a
   // program can actually reach. VIOLATED means the array carries a preparation
   // predicate that was withdrawn, and nothing in api30 withdraws
   // `preparedKeyMaterial`, so its code has no execution path; it is written anyway,
   // for the reason the sibling files record: a code that names a site is cheaper to
   // have and not fire than to add later, and the reason is on the record rather than
   // in the reader's head.
   //
   // The read now asks what the rule requires. api30's SecretKeySpec REQUIRES
   // `preparedKeyMaterial[keyMaterial]` and the seed asked about `randomized`, a
   // different predicate it had conflated with it -- ledger clause #32, recorded at
   // task 4.10 and un-conflated here with its producer, `SecretKeySpec.mop`'s
   // `getEncoded`, in one commit (tasks 5.10 and 6.1).
   //
   // What that costs is measured and large, and it is stated here rather than
   // discovered downstream. `preparedKeyMaterial` is ENSURED by `Key.getEncoded()` and
   // `SecretKey.getEncoded()` and by nothing else in the whole oracle, so the idiom the
   // corpus is built on -- fill a byte[] from an observed SecureRandom and construct a
   // SecretKeySpec from it -- does not satisfy the clause, and the one that does is
   // `generateKey()` then `getEncoded()` then this constructor. Measured against the
   // tree before the un-conflation, seventeen of the 126 corpus traces change: fifteen
   // draw SECRETKEYSPEC-NOBS-00 here, eleven draw CIPHER-NOBS-00 downstream because a
   // construction that breaks the clause ensures no `generatedKey`, and four draw
   // IVPARAMETERSPEC-NOBS-00 because an encoding is no longer randomised bytes. Every
   // one of those is what api30 states; the labels the traces carried were about
   // ordering and algorithm, written before this clause was read at all
   // (researcher decision, 2026-08-22).
   //
   // `spec` is bound only by the conforming branch, because `@match` turns it into the
   // rule's `generatedKey` and CrySL ensures a predicate only for a use that satisfied
   // the rule. A construction that broke the clause therefore reaches the handler with
   // nothing bound, and the write there is a no-op.
   event c1 after(byte[] keyMaterial, String keyAlgorithm) returning(SecretKeySpec secretKeySpec):
    call(public SecretKeySpec.new(byte[], String))
    && args(keyMaterial, keyAlgorithm) {
      boolean conforms = true;
      // The algorithm clause is read here, in the body, and not in `condition(...)`: a false
      // guard there would suppress the transition and turn a value misuse into an ordering
      // accusation (INV-INS-141). It sets `conforms` on the same footing as the predicate
      // read below, so a bad algorithm also stops the `generatedKey` write at `@match`.
      if (!ConscryptAliasTable.matches("SecretKeySpec", keyAlgorithm, algorithms)) {
          ErrorCollector.instance().addError(new ErrorDescription(ErrorType.UnsafeAlgorithm, "SecretKeySpecSpec", "" + __LOC,
              "v=1 code=SECRETKEYSPEC-ALG-00 ev=" + __EVENTNAME + " obj=SecretKeySpec val='" + q(keyAlgorithm) + "' exp='" + q(String.join(",", algorithms)) + "' msg='expecting one of " + String.join(",", algorithms) + " but found " + keyAlgorithm + "'"));
          conforms = false;
      }
      PredicateVerdict preparedMaterial = PredicateStore.instance().validate(Property.PREPARED_KEY_MATERIAL, keyMaterial);
      if (preparedMaterial == PredicateVerdict.VIOLATED) {
          ErrorCollector.instance().addError(new ErrorDescription(ErrorType.UnsatisfiedConstraint, "SecretKeySpecSpec", "" + __LOC,
              "v=1 code=SECRETKEYSPEC-CONSTR-00 ev=" + __EVENTNAME + " obj=SecretKeySpec val='' exp='prepared key material' msg='the keyMaterial given to SecretKeySpec(byte[], String) carries a preparation predicate the rule does not admit'"));
          conforms = false;
      }
      else if (preparedMaterial == PredicateVerdict.NOT_OBSERVED) {
          ErrorCollector.instance().addError(new ErrorDescription(ErrorType.UnsatisfiedConstraint, "SecretKeySpecSpec", "" + __LOC,
              "v=1 code=SECRETKEYSPEC-NOBS-00 ev=" + __EVENTNAME + " obj=SecretKeySpec val='' exp='prepared key material' msg='the keyMaterial given to SecretKeySpec(byte[], String) was not observed to have been prepared by a Key.getEncoded()'"));
          conforms = false;
      }
      if (conforms) {
          spec = secretKeySpec;
          specAlgorithm = keyAlgorithm;
      }
    }

   // The same fusion over the four-argument constructor. Here the twin `c4` was the
   // exact complement -- `keyMaterial.length < offset + len` against `>=` -- so the
   // two halves partition the join point and the fused body is one `if`/`else` over
   // the rule's only CONSTRAINTS clause, `length(keyMaterial) >= off + len`.
   //
   // This overload now reads the same clause the two-argument one does. api30 states a
   // single REQUIRES, over `keyMaterial`, and binds that object in both events of
   // `Cons := c1 | c2`, so the obligation is the constructor's and not one overload's.
   // Task 4.10 left the site out because the clause it would have translated was the
   // conflated one, and adding a second conflated site would have doubled the work task
   // 5.10 had to undo; with the conflation gone, the site is added here, which is where
   // that task said it would be decided (researcher decision, 2026-08-22).
   //
   // The read costs no symbol: the event already exists and already binds the array. It
   // costs two codes, and they are new numbers rather than the two-argument ones,
   // because a code names a site. Measured, one corpus trace changes --
   // SecretKeySpecSpec-offset.txt, whose material an observed SecureRandom filled and
   // which the clause therefore does not accept.
   //
   // The accusing half is unreachable, and is kept anyway. This is an
   // `after ... returning` advice, and the constructor rejects the combination with
   // an exception before it can return (`offset < 0`, `len < 0` and
   // `key.length - offset < len`, in that order; JDK 21
   // java.base/javax/crypto/spec/SecretKeySpec.java:148-168), so no execution
   // reaches SECRETKEYSPEC-CONSTR-01 and no trace can be written that does. It stays
   // because it is the literal transcription of the clause the oracle states, and
   // because the API enforcing a rule's constraint itself is a fact about the
   // platform, not a reason for the specification to stop stating it.
   event c2 after(byte[] keyMaterial, int offset, int len, String keyAlgorithm) returning(SecretKeySpec secretKeySpec):
    call(public SecretKeySpec.new(byte[], int, int, String))
    && args(keyMaterial, offset, len, keyAlgorithm) {
      boolean conforms = true;
      // The rule binds `keyAlgorithm` in both constructors, so the clause is the
      // constructor's and not one overload's; the code is a new number because a code names
      // a site.
      if (!ConscryptAliasTable.matches("SecretKeySpec", keyAlgorithm, algorithms)) {
          ErrorCollector.instance().addError(new ErrorDescription(ErrorType.UnsafeAlgorithm, "SecretKeySpecSpec", "" + __LOC,
              "v=1 code=SECRETKEYSPEC-ALG-01 ev=" + __EVENTNAME + " obj=SecretKeySpec val='" + q(keyAlgorithm) + "' exp='" + q(String.join(",", algorithms)) + "' msg='expecting one of " + String.join(",", algorithms) + " but found " + keyAlgorithm + "'"));
          conforms = false;
      }
      if (keyMaterial.length < offset + len) {
          ErrorCollector.instance().addError(new ErrorDescription(ErrorType.UnsatisfiedConstraint, "SecretKeySpecSpec", "" + __LOC,
              "v=1 code=SECRETKEYSPEC-CONSTR-01 ev=" + __EVENTNAME + " obj=SecretKeySpec val='" + keyMaterial.length + "' exp='at least offset + len' msg='keyMaterial.length is smaller than offset + len'"));
          conforms = false;
      }
      PredicateVerdict preparedMaterial = PredicateStore.instance().validate(Property.PREPARED_KEY_MATERIAL, keyMaterial);
      if (preparedMaterial == PredicateVerdict.VIOLATED) {
          ErrorCollector.instance().addError(new ErrorDescription(ErrorType.UnsatisfiedConstraint, "SecretKeySpecSpec", "" + __LOC,
              "v=1 code=SECRETKEYSPEC-CONSTR-02 ev=" + __EVENTNAME + " obj=SecretKeySpec val='' exp='prepared key material' msg='the keyMaterial given to SecretKeySpec(byte[], int, int, String) carries a preparation predicate the rule does not admit'"));
          conforms = false;
      }
      else if (preparedMaterial == PredicateVerdict.NOT_OBSERVED) {
          ErrorCollector.instance().addError(new ErrorDescription(ErrorType.UnsatisfiedConstraint, "SecretKeySpecSpec", "" + __LOC,
              "v=1 code=SECRETKEYSPEC-NOBS-01 ev=" + __EVENTNAME + " obj=SecretKeySpec val='' exp='prepared key material' msg='the keyMaterial given to SecretKeySpec(byte[], int, int, String) was not observed to have been prepared by a Key.getEncoded()'"));
          conforms = false;
      }
      if (conforms) {
          spec = secretKeySpec;
          specAlgorithm = keyAlgorithm;
      }
    }

   // `Cons` is the whole of the rule's ORDER, and `Cons := c1 | c2`: constructing a
   // SecretKeySpec is the entire ordering this specification observes. So the `@fail`
   // handler below cannot fire. Measured in the generated monitor: the transition row
   // is {1, 2, 2} for both events and the monitor is keyed on the constructed object,
   // so no monitor of this specification ever sees a second event and state 2 is never
   // entered. SECRETKEYSPEC-ORDER-00 is therefore a third code with no execution path,
   // beside SECRETKEYSPEC-CONSTR-01 and the VIOLATED branch of `c1`. This is the
   // position every constructor-only specification of the set is in, and the repair is
   // to the automaton: recorded here, owned by the task that repairs the family.
   ere : c1 | c2

   @fail {
      ErrorCollector.instance().addError(new ErrorDescription(ErrorType.InvalidSequenceOfMethodCalls, "SecretKeySpecSpec", "" + __LOC,
          "v=1 code=SECRETKEYSPEC-ORDER-00 ev=" + __EVENTNAME + " obj=SecretKeySpec val='' exp='' msg='the observed call sequence is not one SecretKeySpecSpec accepts'"));
      __RESET;
   }

   // The one place the rule's `ENSURES generatedKey[this, alg]` may be written. api30
   // states the clause without an `after L` qualification, so its acceptance point is
   // the accepting state itself, which in an `ere` is exactly what `@match` names.
   //
   // The write is now at the arity the rule states, two places over the object and its
   // algorithm, and it rises here in the same commit as both of its readers -- which is
   // the condition task 4.10 recorded when it left the write at one place. `validate`
   // compares the value tuple, so a two-place write read by a one-place `validate`
   // returns VIOLATED, not NOT_OBSERVED, and raising either side alone would turn every
   // SecretKeySpec-created key given to `Cipher.init` into a positive accusation about a
   // program that conforms. The readers that rise with it are `CipherSpec.i2`, which
   // applies the rule's `part(0,"/",transformation)` splitter, and `SecretKeySpec.e1`,
   // which asks with the key's own algorithm.
   //
   // `SecretKeySpecSpec-cipher-chain.txt` is the trace that measured exactly this at
   // task 5.6, and it no longer measures it from silence: its material comes from a
   // SecureRandom, so this pass's read refuses the construction and the write below
   // never happens for it. What the trace still pins is the arity -- were only one side
   // two-place, a construction the clause does accept would draw CIPHER-CONSTR-00
   // instead of CIPHER-NOBS-00 -- and SecretKeySpecSpec-prepared-material.txt is the
   // trace that carries the chain through from silence now.
   //
   // The rule's other ENSURES, `speccedKey[this, _]`, is not written at all. Its
   // consumer is SecretKeyFactory, which has no specification in this set; ledger #31
   // records the chain as `unmonitored-consumer` at task 5.10, and PBEKeySpec already
   // produces the predicate. A second producer for a chain with no consumer would be a
   // site with no purpose, and no read is fabricated to give it one.
   //
   // The accepting-state bookkeeping that stood beside this write is gone: the new
   // store does not offer it, and the set never read it back (INV-INS-147).
   @match {
      PredicateStore.instance().ensure(Property.GENERATED_KEY, spec, specAlgorithm);
   }

}
